.TH STTY 1
.SH NAME
stty \- set terminal options
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B stty
[ option ... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Stty
sets certain I/O options on the current output terminal.
With no argument, it reports the current settings of the options.
The report goes to the standard error.
The option strings are
selected from the following set:
.TP  8n
.B raw
raw mode input
(no erase, kill, interrupt, quit, EOT)
.br
.ns
.TP
.B \-raw
negate raw mode
.br
.ns
.TP
.B cooked
same as `\-raw'
.br
.ns
.TP
.B cbreak
make each character available to
.IR read (2)
as received; no erase and kill
.br
.ns
.TP
.B \-cbreak
make characters available to
.I read
only when newline is received
.br
.ns
.TP
.B \-nl
allow carriage return for new-line,
and output CR-LF for carriage return or new-line
.br
.ns
.TP
.B nl
accept only new-line to end lines
.br
.ns
.TP
.B echo
echo back every character typed
.br
.ns
.TP
.B \-echo
do not echo characters
.br
.ns
.TP
.B \-tabs
replace tabs by spaces when printing
.br
.ns
.TP
.B tabs
preserve tabs
.br
.ns
.TP
.B ek
reset erase and kill characters back to normal # and @
.br
.ns
.TP
.BI erase  \ c\fR
set erase character to
.IR c .
.I C
can be of the form
.I `^X'
which is interpreted as a `control X'.
.br
.ns
.TP
.BI kill  \ c\fR
set kill character to
.IR c .
.I `^X'
works here also.
.PP
That is the whole list, and it is much shorter than v7's.
.PP
.B Note:
the options this page used to name that describe hardware the BESM-6
has not got were removed from the program rather than left to fail
quietly, on the precedent
.IR getty (8)
set when it cut its speed table to one entry.
Each was measured against the driver, not assumed:
.TP  8n
.B "50 \&... 9600 exta extb 0"
the baud rates.
A Consul-254 is a parallel typewriter that moves one whole character
per instruction; there is no line whose rate could be set.
.I sg_ispeed
and
.I sg_ospeed
are stored and handed back by the kernel and read by nothing.
.br
.ns
.TP
.B "even \-even odd \-odd"
parity.
The Consul line carries eight bits of data end to end.
.br
.ns
.TP
.B "cr0\-cr3 nl0\-nl3 tab0\-tab3 ff0 ff1 bs0 bs1"
the delays.
Nothing on this terminal has a carriage to wait for, and on an
eight-bit line a queued byte above 0177 is data and cannot also be a
delay count.
.B \-tabs
is the exception and remains: tab expansion is done by column and is live.
.br
.ns
.TP
.B "tty33 tty37 vt05 tn300 ti700 tek"
terminals that are not this one; each was only a bundle of the delays above.
.br
.ns
.TP
.B "lcase \-lcase"
case mapping.
The kernel does implement it, in both directions \- and it would undo
the driver, whose line is raw because the Consul's own code has no
lower-case Latin at all.
The text of this system is UTF-8 end to end.
.br
.ns
.TP
.B "hup \-hup"
.IR ioctl (2)'s
TIOCHPCL sets a bit that nothing in this kernel ever tests.
It was this program's only
.I ioctl
call.
.PP
.B Note:
.B \-hup
and
.B "\&0"
were on this page and were never in v7's program.
.PP
.B Note:
TANDEM, the flow-control flag, is live in the kernel and this program
still cannot set it \- v7's had no word for it either.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
`stty: not a terminal' if the standard output is not one.
v7 ignored
.IR gtty (2)'s
return and reported the modes of a zeroed structure instead.
.PP
An unrecognised word gives `unknown mode: \fIword\fR' and the
remaining options are still applied, which is v7's behaviour.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
ioctl(2), tty(4), getty(8)
